      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, we need to calculate how many pairs of these points can uniquely define a plane. Each plane is formed by three non-collinear points. Therefore, the problem reduces to finding the number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12, which is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of points, and \\( k \\) is the number of points to choose.\n\nIn this case, \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\). The formula is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 9! \\) in the numerator and denominator cancel each other out, we are left with:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12}{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 220\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum number of planes that can be uniquely determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
