      "text": "To determine the maximum number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, we need to consider how many unique sets of three points can be chosen from the 12 points. This can be modeled by finding the number of combinations of 12 points taken 3 at a time.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\\[\nC(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\nIn our case, \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\). Thus, the number of planes that can be determined by combining 3 points from 12 is calculated as follows:\n\\[\nC(12, 3) = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the factorials:\n\\[\n12! = 12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!\n\\]\n\nCancel out the common \\(9!\\) term:\n\\[\n\\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!}\n\\]\n\nCancel the \\(9!\\):\n\\[\n= \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNote that \\(3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\\):\n\\[\n= \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the numerator:\n\\[\n12 \\times 11 \\times 10 = 1320\n\\]\n\nThen divide by the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
